Cell["\<\
In the following we will refer to the magnitude (gain) and phase of the \
impedance transfer function. Together they allow us to generate the \
electrical impedance Bode plot by substitution the Laplace, s, by \
\[ImaginaryI]\[Omega]  :
\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.7927584286155887`*^9, 3.79275843435165*^9}, {
  3.792784612314693*^9, 
  3.7927846949886703`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"ede8325d-0511-4d43-be43-\
9c255392a54e"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"gain", "[", 
   RowBox[{"z", "[", "s_", "]"}], "]"}], ":=", " ", 
  SqrtBox[
   RowBox[{
    SuperscriptBox[
     RowBox[{"Re", "[", 
      RowBox[{"z", "[", "s", "]"}], "]"}], "2"], "+", 
    SuperscriptBox[
     RowBox[{"Im", "[", 
      RowBox[{"s", "[", "s", "]"}], "]"}], "2"]}]]}]], "Input",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.792758535385184*^9, 3.792758610085106*^9}, {
  3.792758644744296*^9, 3.7927586524005785`*^9}},
 CellLabel->"In[3]:=",ExpressionUUID->"1dfab868-1126-4a54-8bb1-8feb6e68ac06"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"phase", "[", 
   RowBox[{"z", "[", "s_", "]"}], "]"}], ":=", 
  RowBox[{"ArcTan", "[", 
   FractionBox[
    RowBox[{"Im", "[", 
     RowBox[{"z", "[", "s", "]"}], "]"}], 
    RowBox[{"Re", "[", 
     RowBox[{"z", "[", "s", "]"}], "]"}]], "]"}]}]], "Input",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.792758657510588*^9, 3.792758743093503*^9}, {
  3.797769055274131*^9, 3.797769057841286*^9}},
 CellLabel->"In[4]:=",ExpressionUUID->"eaeabcc7-1c44-4e12-aa70-9600ff5f2faf"],

Cell["\<\
Note that in Mathematica (and as used below) we can use Abs[] and Arg[] to \
determine the gain[] and phase[] respectively. Note also that the phase is \
plotted in degrees rather than radians. Arg[] returns values in radians so to \
convert to degrees we multiply by  360/(2*Pi). In addition we show frequency \
in Hz rather than radians/s and so we substitute, s, by  \[ImaginaryI] \
\[Omega] 2\[Pi] .

Note: electrical impedance is measured over a range of frequencies. The \
question arises as to what that range should be. For example if your are \
measuring the electrical impedance of components of an audio system then \
given the human range of hearing goes from 20 Hz to 20 kHz you might cover \
that range (and perhaps a little more e.g., 10 Hz to 100 kHz) when measuring \
the electrical impedance of those components (including loud speakers).\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.797769098494548*^9, 3.797769179460994*^9}, {
  3.797769307064822*^9, 3.797769312605055*^9}, {3.7977693504119496`*^9, 
  3.7977694182769136`*^9}, {3.797769454963011*^9, 3.797769515382829*^9}, {
  3.797769556500864*^9, 3.797769581637147*^9}, {3.797770222855869*^9, 
  3.79777034559015*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"c347759e-33ff-4b1e-95e7-\
370e85ad46ff"],

Cell["Lets now consider some common components.", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.792758759316969*^9, 
  3.79275878374821*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"bdf1d520-59c9-4f5b-9b7e-\
7c6209ad8c49"]
}, Open  ]],

