Cell["\<\
Notice that the phase of an ideal resistor is 0 deg.
Therefor for an ideal resistor the impedance Bode plot gain has a slope of 0 \
and a phase of 0 deg.
An ideal resistor cannot store electrical energy.
\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.792766733796895*^9, 3.792766812161793*^9}, {
  3.792767335113457*^9, 3.792767392072953*^9}, {3.7927674269593925`*^9, 
  3.792767431118195*^9}, {3.7927681263530445`*^9, 3.7927681629247303`*^9}, {
  3.792768775576348*^9, 3.792768776045039*^9}, {3.792770055809559*^9, 
  3.792770058294434*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"9da497ae-e970-41fb-8ad5-\
349875cacb35"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Real Resistor", "Subsubsection",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.92178493944515*^9, 
  3.9217849434045687`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"f547ba8a-c8db-4313-9903-\
041a3e7b989e"],

Cell["\<\
We now will measure the impedance of a real 10 k\[CapitalOmega] metal film \
resistor using the Digilent Discovery 3 fitted with the Digilent Impedance \
module. Make sure that the component under test (the 10 k\[CapitalOmega] \
resistor here) is inserted fully in to the Digilent Impedance Module \
component clamps. Set up the parameters of the impedance sweep as shown in \
the image below:\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.92178495047714*^9, 3.921785039284881*^9}, {
  3.9217851131104*^9, 3.921785174269658*^9}, {3.9217874930222893`*^9, 
  3.92178758834803*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"7363eb4d-e7ab-4257-a6ec-\
