  3.792759394773674*^9, {3.792759467710343*^9, 3.792759472915758*^9}, 
   3.7927698242724037`*^9, {3.792769886865552*^9, 3.792769887646931*^9}, {
   3.792783812843458*^9, 3.7927838316897917`*^9}, {3.792834718073238*^9, 
   3.792834725059765*^9}},
 TextAlignment->Center,ExpressionUUID->"02af11cc-3abc-4bc1-b748-f0f7a0e33626"],

Cell["\<\
The Farad (F) is the unit of capacitance. The symbol C and circuit symbol \
shown above are often used in reference to capacitors. The image shows a 680 \
nF capacitor. There are many types of capacitors (e.g., ceramic, silver mica, \
polycarbonate, polystyrene, tantalum). Tantalum capacitors only behave as \
ideal capacitors over a narrow range of frequencies and are usually used for \
\[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]bypassing\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] power supply voltage \
fluctuations on printed circuit boards (PCBs). Silver mica capacitors are \
amazing and behave like pure capacitors over a wide range of frequencies but \
they are very expensive.
Depending on the type of material used in the capacitor construction \
capacitors are commonly available from 1 pF to 100 \[Mu]F. Low voltage super \
capacitors are readily available up 3000 F (size of a beverage can).\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.79275969687576*^9, 3.7927597880323486`*^9}, {
   3.792759845375501*^9, 3.792759868726309*^9}, {3.792759967466978*^9, 
   3.792759984203526*^9}, {3.792760142748479*^9, 3.7927601972433395`*^9}, {
   3.79276028405122*^9, 3.792760386261202*^9}, {3.792769099869158*^9, 
   3.792769125389396*^9}, {3.792769325411181*^9, 3.7927694567128906`*^9}, {
   3.7927699082285657`*^9, 3.792769932078782*^9}, {3.792769988719988*^9, 
   3.792769990484886*^9}, {3.79278483793681*^9, 3.7927849319760857`*^9}, {
   3.7928351235142303`*^9, 3.792835185116901*^9}, 3.921786246026959*^9, {
   3.9508659706572866`*^9, 
   3.950866029610898*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"cfedffae-ff68-43fd-b825-\
d52286d9f907"],

